{"name":"Amok","hex":"#ddcc22","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"amok","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Out of control, especially when armed and dangerous.","In a frenzy of violence, or on a killing spree; berserk."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One who runs amok; in Malay and Moro/Philippine culture, one who attempts to kill many others, especially expecting that they will be killed themselves.","The act of running amok."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/amok"}],"entries":[{"title":"Amok syndrome","blocks":[{"text":"Amok syndrome is an aggressive dissociative behavioral pattern derived from the Malay world which led to the English phrase running amok. The word derives from the Malay word amuk, traditionally meaning \"rushing in a frenzy\" or \"attacking furiously\". Amok syndrome presents as an episode of sudden mass assault against people or objects following a period of brooding. It has traditionally been regarded as specifically occurring in Malay culture, but is now increasingly viewed as more general psychopathological behavior. The syndrome of \"Amok\" is found in the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders. In the DSM-V, Amok syndrome is no longer considered a culture-bound syndrome, since the category of culture-bound syndrome has been removed."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Amok_%28effet_de_l%E2%80%99opium_sur_les_Malais%29._Wood_cut%2C_1864%2C_drawing_by_de_Molins_and_Doerr._Illustr._to_De_Molins%2C_Voyage_a_Java_1858%E2%80%931861_from_Le_tour_du_monde_nouveau_journal_des_voyages_Volume_10.jpg/330px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amok_syndrome","wikidata":"Q473814"},{"title":"Amok (1934 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Amok is a 1934 French film, directed by Fyodor Otsep. The director was nominated for the Mussolini Cup at the 1934 Venice International Film Festival. The film is based on the Stefan Zweig novella Amok (novella)."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amok_(1934_film)","wikidata":"Q4747481"},{"title":"Amok (novella)","blocks":[{"text":"Amok is a novella by the Austrian author Stefan Zweig. First printed in the newspaper Neue Freie Presse in 1922, Amok appeared shortly afterwards in the collection of novellas Amok: Novellas of a Passion. As Zweig was fascinated and influenced by Sigmund Freud's work,  Amok includes clear psychoanalytic elements. It deals with an extreme obsession, which leads the protagonist to sacrifice his professional and private life and, eventually, to commit suicide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Zweigamok.jpg/330px-Zweigamok.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amok_(novella)","wikidata":"Q675609"},{"title":"Amok (Atoms for Peace album)","blocks":[{"text":"Amok is the only album by the rock band Atoms for Peace, released on February 25, 2013, by XL Recordings. It features the Radiohead singer Thom Yorke, the Radiohead producer Nigel Godrich, the Red Hot Chili Peppers bassist Flea, the drummer Joey Waronker and the percussionist Mauro Refosco. It combines electronic and live instrumentation."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/2/25/Atomsforamok.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amok_(Atoms_for_Peace_album)","wikidata":"Q3180636"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Amok%2C_furious_madness_of_the_Malays._The_thorns_penetrate_the_flesh_and_paralyze_him.jpg/500px-Amok%2C_furious_madness_of_the_Malays._The_thorns_penetrate_the_flesh_and_paralyze_him.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Amok%2C_furious_madness_of_the_Malays._The_thorns_penetrate_the_flesh_and_paralyze_him.jpg/1280px-Amok%2C_furious_madness_of_the_Malays._The_thorns_penetrate_the_flesh_and_paralyze_him.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Amok%2C_furious_madness_of_the_Malays._The_thorns_penetrate_the_flesh_and_paralyze_him.jpg/500px-Amok%2C_furious_madness_of_the_Malays._The_thorns_penetrate_the_flesh_and_paralyze_him.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A pengamok being captured, 1883. The thorns on the pole paralyze him.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmok%2C_furious_madness_of_the_Malays._The_thorns_penetrate_the_flesh_and_paralyze_him.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Native_Policemen_in_Java_1911.jpg/500px-Native_Policemen_in_Java_1911.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Native_Policemen_in_Java_1911.jpg/500px-Native_Policemen_in_Java_1911.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Native policemen in Java, 1911. The man in the centre is holding a sangga mara, a 2-forked pole for catching amok.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANative_Policemen_in_Java_1911.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Melt_Festival_2013_-_Atoms_For_Peace-5.jpg/500px-Melt_Festival_2013_-_Atoms_For_Peace-5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Melt_Festival_2013_-_Atoms_For_Peace-5.jpg/1280px-Melt_Festival_2013_-_Atoms_For_Peace-5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Melt_Festival_2013_-_Atoms_For_Peace-5.jpg/500px-Melt_Festival_2013_-_Atoms_For_Peace-5.jpg?utm_source=en.